# Verify a release attestation

Check that a file on PyPI was built and published by this project's own {% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}workflow{% else %}pipeline{% endif %}, and not
uploaded by anyone else who got hold of a credential. Do it after your first release to confirm
the setup, and whenever someone asks whether a release is genuine.

Every release published through Trusted Publishing carries a [PEP 740](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/specifications/index-hosted-attestations/)
attestation per wheel and sdist, signed by `release.attest` in the publish job.
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %} The GitHub publish
job also records a second, GitHub-native provenance attestation; both are covered below.
{%- endif %} Releases published with `UV_PUBLISH_*` credentials carry none; see
[Set up Trusted Publishing](set-up-trusted-publishing.md).

## On PyPI's website

Open the release on PyPI, go to *Download files*, and pick a file's *view details*. The
*Provenance* section names the repository, the {% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}workflow file{% else %}pipeline file{% endif %} and the commit that published it.
A file without that section was not uploaded with an attestation.

## From the command line

`pypi-attestations` is in the project's `security` group, so `uv run` has it:

```bash
uv run pypi-attestations verify pypi \
  --repository <repository URL> \
  pypi:{{ project_slug }}-<version>-py3-none-any.whl
```

`<repository URL>` is the https URL of this repository{% if repository_url %} (`{{ repository_url | trim | regex_replace('(\\.git)?/*$', '') }}`){% endif %}.
It downloads the file and its attestation from PyPI and checks that the signature is valid, that
it covers this exact file, and that the signer is a workflow in that repository. Success prints
`OK:` and the file name; any mismatch fails with the reason.

To check a file you already downloaded, pass its path instead of `pypi:<filename>`.

To see what an attestation in `dist/` says before uploading, for instance after running
`release.attest` locally:

```bash
uv run pypi-attestations inspect dist/{{ project_slug }}-<version>-py3-none-any.whl.publish.attestation
```
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}

## GitHub's provenance attestation

The publish job's *Attest build provenance* step records a second attestation with GitHub, over
the same wheel and sdist. Check a downloaded file against it with the
[GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com/):

```bash
gh attestation verify {{ project_slug }}-<version>-py3-none-any.whl --repo <owner>/<repo>
```

It answers the same question as the PEP 740 check. The difference is where the attestation is
stored: with the repository on GitHub, instead of with the package on PyPI.
{%- endif %}

## When verification fails

- **No attestation found:** the release was published with credentials, or before this project
  signed attestations. Nothing is wrong with the file; there is just nothing to verify.
- **Identity mismatch:** the `--repository` you passed is not the one that signed. Check for a
  fork, a renamed repository, or a typo. If the repository is right, treat the file as untrusted.
- **Digest mismatch:** the file is not the one that was signed. Do not install it.

